{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-001", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Six-digit numbers with digit sum 27", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 4885, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 297, "reasoning_tokens": 98, "input_tokens": 45, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 593, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "55252", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "55252"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d7c40eb-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351c-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:06-0500", "answer": "Write each integer as a six-digit string, allowing leading zeros. This includes 000000, but its digit sum is not 27, so it does not affect the count.\n\nWe count solutions to\n\\[\nd_1+\\cdots+d_6=27,\\qquad 0\\le d_i\\le 9.\n\\]\nWithout the upper bounds, stars and bars gives \\(\\binom{32}{5}\\) solutions.\n\nBy inclusion\u2013exclusion, subtract solutions where a digit is at least 10, then add back those where two digits are at least 10. Three such digits would already sum to at least 30. Thus the count is\n\\[\n\\binom{32}{5}-6\\binom{22}{5}+\\binom62\\binom{12}{5}\n=201376-158004+11880\n=55252.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 55252"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-001", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Six-digit numbers with digit sum 27", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 5011, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 784, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 44, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 2006, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "55252", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "55252"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d7c40e9-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351c-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:06-0500", "answer": "I need to find the number of integers n with 1 \u2264 n \u2264 999999 whose decimal digits sum to exactly 27.\n\nSince 999999 has 6 digits, I need to count numbers from 1 to 999999 with digit sum 27. This is equivalent to counting 6-digit strings (allowing leading zeros) where digits sum to 27, then subtracting the case where all digits are 0 (which has sum 0, not 27, so no adjustment needed for that).\n\nActually, let me be more careful. Numbers from 1 to 999999 can be represented as 6-digit strings with leading zeros allowed (from 000001 to 999999). The number 000000 = 0 is excluded, but it has digit sum 0, not 27, so it doesn't affect our count.\n\nSo I need: number of solutions to x\u2081 + x\u2082 + x\u2083 + x\u2084 + x\u2085 + x\u2086 = 27 where 0 \u2264 x\u1d62 \u2264 9 for each i.\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion. First, count non-negative integer solutions without the upper bound: C(27+6-1, 6-1) = C(32, 5).\n\nNow subtract cases where some x\u1d62 \u2265 10. If x\u1d62 \u2265 10, let y\u1d62 = x\u1d62 - 10 \u2265 0. Then we need y\u1d62 + sum of others = 17, giving C(17+6-1, 5) = C(22, 5) solutions for each variable that exceeds 9. There are 6 choices for which variable, so subtract 6\u00b7C(22, 5).\n\nAdd back cases where two variables \u2265 10: then sum of (x\u1d62-10) + (x\u2c7c-10) + others = 7, so C(7+6-1, 5) = C(12, 5). There are C(6,2) = 15 ways to choose two variables, so add 15\u00b7C(12, 5).\n\nSubtract cases where three variables \u2265 10: sum would be 27 - 30 = -3 < 0, impossible. So we stop.\n\nLet me compute:\n- C(32, 5) = 32!/(5!\u00b727!) = (32\u00b731\u00b730\u00b729\u00b728)/(120) = (32\u00b731\u00b730\u00b729\u00b728)/120\n\n32\u00b731 = 992\n992\u00b730 = 29760\n29760\u00b729 = 863040\n863040\u00b728 = 24165120\n24165120/120 = 201376\n\n- C(22, 5) = (22\u00b721\u00b720\u00b719\u00b718)/120 = (22\u00b721\u00b720\u00b719\u00b718)/120\n22\u00b721 = 462\n462\u00b720 = 9240\n9240\u00b719 = 175560\n175560\u00b718 = 3160080\n3160080/120 = 26334\n\n- C(12, 5) = (12\u00b711\u00b710\u00b79\u00b78)/120 = 95040/120 = 792\n\nSo the count is:\nC(32,5) - 6\u00b7C(22,5) + 15\u00b7C(12,5)\n= 201376 - 6\u00b726334 + 15\u00b7792\n= 201376 - 158004 + 11880\n= 201376 - 158004 = 43372\n43372 + 11880 = 55252\n\nLet me verify: 201376 - 158004 = 43372. And 43372 + 11880 = 55252.\n\nANSWER: 55252"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-005", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Divisible by exactly two of 4, 6 and 9", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 4524, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 327, "reasoning_tokens": 99, "input_tokens": 56, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 609, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "83334", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "83334"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5e4da9b5-d02a-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423520-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:06-0500", "answer": "The pairwise least common multiples are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{lcm}(4,6)=12,\\quad \\operatorname{lcm}(4,9)=36,\\quad \\operatorname{lcm}(6,9)=18.\n\\]\nThe least common multiple of all three is \\(36\\).\n\nAdding the counts of multiples of the pairwise LCMs counts each integer divisible by exactly two once, but each integer divisible by all three three times. Thus the required count is\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{12}\\right\\rfloor+\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{36}\\right\\rfloor+\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{18}\\right\\rfloor-\n3\\left\\lfloor\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{36}\\right\\rfloor\n=83{,}334.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 83334"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-002", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Seven-seat row logic puzzle", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6218, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 331, "reasoning_tokens": 187, "input_tokens": 172, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 433, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d69f158-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351d-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:06-0500", "answer": "The seating order is **Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus**.\n\nChecking the clues:\n- Ben (3) and Dev (6) have exactly two people between them.\n- Eli (1) and Gus (7) are not adjacent, and Gus is to Eli\u2019s right.\n- Cai (2) sits to the left of Fay (5), who sits to the left of Dev (6).\n- Ana (4) and Eli (1) have exactly two people between them.\n- Ana (4) sits immediately to the left of Fay (5).\n\nANSWER: Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}
