{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-001", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Six-digit numbers with digit sum 27", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6713, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 403, "reasoning_tokens": 208, "input_tokens": 45, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 582, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "55252", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "55252"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d7c40ed-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351c-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:07-0500", "answer": "Pad each integer with leading zeros to make a six-digit string. We need to count solutions to\n\\[\nd_1+\\cdots+d_6=27,\\qquad 0\\le d_i\\le9.\n\\]\n(The all-zero string has sum \\(0\\), so it is not counted.)\n\nWithout the upper bounds, stars and bars gives \\(\\binom{32}{5}\\) solutions. By inclusion\u2013exclusion, subtract solutions where a chosen digit is at least \\(10\\), and add back those where two chosen digits are at least \\(10\\). Three such digits would already sum to at least \\(30\\), so\n\\[\n\\binom{32}{5}-6\\binom{22}{5}+\\binom62\\binom{12}{5}\n=201376-158004+11880\n=55252.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 55252"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-004", "category": "reasoning", "title": "New York to London across the DST gap", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 2415, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 128, "reasoning_tokens": 45, "input_tokens": 105, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 243, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5eefc8ce-d02a-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351e-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:07-0500", "answer": "New York starts daylight saving time on March 8, 2026; London starts on March 29. London is therefore five hours ahead on March 2 and March 30, but four hours ahead for the three intervening meetings.\n\nANSWER: 14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-005", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Divisible by exactly two of 4, 6 and 9", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6153, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1168, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 55, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 2691, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "83334", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "83334"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5e4da9b4-d02a-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423520-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:08-0500", "answer": "I need to find integers n in [1, 1,000,000] divisible by exactly two of {4, 6, 9}.\n\nFirst, let me find the LCMs:\n- lcm(4, 6) = 12\n- lcm(4, 9) = 36\n- lcm(6, 9) = 18\n- lcm(4, 6, 9) = 36\n\nLet me define:\n- A = divisible by 4\n- B = divisible by 6\n- C = divisible by 9\n\nI want |A\u2229B\u2229C'| + |A\u2229B'\u2229C| + |A'\u2229B\u2229C| (exactly two)\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion for \"exactly two\":\n- |A\u2229B| - |A\u2229B\u2229C| = divisible by 12 but not 36\n- |A\u2229C| - |A\u2229B\u2229C| = divisible by 36 but not... wait, A\u2229C = divisible by lcm(4,9) = 36, and A\u2229B\u2229C = divisible by 36. So |A\u2229C| - |A\u2229B\u2229C| = 0?\n\nLet me check: if n is divisible by 4 and 9, then n is divisible by 36. Is n divisible by 6? Since 36 = 4\u00b79 = 2\u00b2\u00b73\u00b2, and 6 = 2\u00b73, yes! So A\u2229C \u2286 B, meaning A\u2229C = A\u2229B\u2229C.\n\nSo:\n- Exactly A and B (not C): divisible by 12 but not by 36\n- Exactly A and C (not B): impossible (empty set)\n- Exactly B and C (not A): divisible by 18 but not by 36? Wait, need to check: divisible by 6 and 9 means divisible by 18. Not divisible by 4.\n\nLet me verify: if n is divisible by 6 and 9, then n is divisible by lcm(6,9) = 18. For n to not be divisible by 4, we need n divisible by 18 but not by 4.\n\nBut wait - I need to check if divisible by 18 implies anything about 4. 18 = 2\u00b73\u00b2, so n = 18k. For n to be divisible by 4, we need 18k \u2261 0 (mod 4), so 2k \u2261 0 (mod 4), so k \u2261 0 (mod 2). So n divisible by 36.\n\nSo \"divisible by 6 and 9 but not 4\" = \"divisible by 18 but not by 36\".\n\nAnd \"divisible by 4 and 6 but not 9\" = \"divisible by 12 but not by 36\"? Check: if divisible by 12 = 4\u00b73 = 2\u00b2\u00b73, need not divisible by 9 = 3\u00b2. So yes, divisible by 12 but not by 36 (since 36 = 4\u00b79).\n\nWait, let me verify: divisible by 12 but not 36. If n = 12k and 36 \u2224 n, then 3 \u2224 k, so n has exactly one factor of 3. So n not divisible by 9. \u2713\n\nAnd \"divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6\": impossible since 4 and 9 implies 36 implies 6.\n\nSo I need:\n- Count of n divisible by 12 but not 36\n- Count of n divisible by 18 but not 36\n\nCount divisible by 12 but not 36:\n- \u230a1,000,000/12\u230b - \u230a1,000,000/36\u230b = 83,333 - 27,777 = 55,556\n\nLet me compute: 1,000,000/12 = 83,333.33..., so 83,333\n1,000,000/36 = 27,777.77..., so 27,777\nDifference: 83,333 - 27,777 = 55,556\n\nCount divisible by 18 but not 36:\n- \u230a1,000,000/18\u230b - \u230a1,000,000/36\u230b = 55,555 - 27,777 = 27,778\n\nLet me compute: 1,000,000/18 = 55,555.55..., so 55,555\n1,000,000/36 = 27,777.77..., so 27,777\nDifference: 55,555 - 27,777 = 27,778\n\nTotal: 55,556 + 27,778 = 83,334\n\nLet me double-check by computing directly.\n\nDivisible by 12: 83,333\nDivisible by 18: 55,555\nDivisible by 36: 27,777\n\nDivisible by 12 but not 36: 83,333 - 27,777 = 55,556\nDivisible by 18 but not 36: 55,555 - 27,777 = 27,778\n\nTotal: 55,556 + 27,778 = 83,334\n\nANSWER: 83334"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-002", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Seven-seat row logic puzzle", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 7569, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1376, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 176, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3401, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d6a186c-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351d-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:08-0500", "answer": "I need to find the seating order for seven people based on the given clues.\n\nLet me list the clues:\n1. Exactly two people sit between Ben and Dev (so positions differ by 3)\n2. Eli and Gus do not sit next to each other\n3. Dev sits somewhere to the right of Fay (Dev > Fay)\n4. Fay sits somewhere to the right of Cai (Fay > Cai)\n5. Gus sits somewhere to the right of Eli (Gus > Eli)\n6. Exactly two people sit between Ana and Eli (positions differ by 3)\n7. Ana sits immediately to the left of Fay (Ana = Fay - 1)\n\nFrom clue 7: Ana and Fay are adjacent, with Ana immediately left of Fay. So they occupy positions (k, k+1) for some k.\n\nFrom clue 4: Cai < Fay, so Cai is to the left of Fay.\nFrom clue 3: Fay < Dev, so Dev is to the right of Fay.\n\nSo we have: Cai ... Ana, Fay ... Dev (with Ana immediately left of Fay)\n\nFrom clue 6: Ana and Eli have exactly two people between them, so |Ana - Eli| = 3.\n\nSince Ana is at position k, Eli is at k-3 or k+3.\n\nFrom clue 5: Gus > Eli (Gus is to the right of Eli)\nFrom clue 2: Eli and Gus are not adjacent.\n\nLet me try possibilities. Ana and Fay are at (k, k+1).\n\nCase: k = 1, so Ana=1, Fay=2. Then Cai < 2, so Cai must be... but there's no seat left of 1. Invalid.\n\nCase: k = 2, so Ana=2, Fay=3. Then Cai < 3, so Cai = 1.\nThen Dev > 3, so Dev \u2208 {4,5,6,7}.\nEli is at 2-3 = -1 (invalid) or 2+3 = 5. So Eli = 5.\nGus > 5, so Gus \u2208 {6,7}. But Eli=5, so Gus can't be 6 (adjacent). So Gus = 7.\nCheck clue 1: Ben and Dev have exactly two between them. Dev \u2208 {4,6} (not 5,7 taken).\nIf Dev = 4: Ben is at 4-3=1 or 4+3=7. Seat 1 is Cai, seat 7 is Gus. Neither works.\nIf Dev = 6: Ben is at 6-3=3 or 6+3=9. Seat 3 is Fay. Invalid.\nSo k=2 doesn't work.\n\nCase: k = 3, so Ana=3, Fay=4. Then Cai < 4, so Cai \u2208 {1,2}.\nEli is at 3-3=0 (invalid) or 3+3=6. So Eli = 6.\nGus > 6, so Gus = 7. But then Eli=6 and Gus=7 are adjacent! Violates clue 2.\nSo k=3 doesn't work.\n\nCase: k = 4, so Ana=4, Fay=5. Then Cai < 5, so Cai \u2208 {1,2,3}.\nEli is at 4-3=1 or 4+3=7.\nSubcase Eli = 1: Gus > 1, and Gus \u2260 2 (not adjacent to Eli=1). So Gus \u2208 {3,4,5,6,7} minus taken. But 4=Ana, 5=Fay. So Gus \u2208 {3,6,7}.\nDev > 5, so Dev \u2208 {6,7}.\nClue 1: Ben and Dev differ by 3.\nIf Dev = 6: Ben = 3 or 9. So Ben = 3. Then Gus \u2208 {7} (since 3,6 taken, and Gus\u22602). Check: Gus=7, Eli=1, not adjacent. Good.\nRemaining seat: 2 for Cai? But Cai < 5, and seat 2 is available. Let's check: Cai=2.\nOrder: 1=Eli, 2=Cai, 3=Ben, 4=Ana, 5=Fay, 6=Dev, 7=Gus.\nCheck all clues:\n- Ben(3) and Dev(6): two between (4,5). \u2713\n- Eli(1) and Gus(7): not adjacent. \u2713\n- Dev(6) > Fay(5). \u2713\n- Fay(5) > Cai(2). \u2713\n- Gus(7) > Eli(1). \u2713\n- Ana(4) and Eli(1): two between (2,3). \u2713\n- Ana(4) immediately left of Fay(5). \u2713\n\nLet me verify this works! All clues satisfied.\n\nSubcase Eli = 7: Gus > 7, impossible.\n\nCase: k = 5, so Ana=5, Fay=6. Then Cai < 6.\nEli is at 5-3=2 or 5+3=8 (invalid). So Eli = 2.\nGus > 2, and Gus \u2260 3 (not adjacent to Eli=2). So Gus \u2208 {4,5,6,7} minus taken. 5=Ana, 6=Fay. So Gus \u2208 {4,7}.\nDev > 6, so Dev = 7. Then Gus = 4.\nClue 1: Ben and Dev(7) differ by 3. So Ben = 4. But Gus = 4. Conflict.\nSo k=5 doesn't work.\n\nCase: k = 6, so Ana=6, Fay=7. Then Cai < 7.\nEli is at 6-3=3 or 6+3=9 (invalid). So Eli = 3.\nGus > 3, and Gus \u2260 4. So Gus \u2208 {5,6,7} minus taken. 6=Ana, 7=Fay. So Gus = 5.\nDev > 7, impossible.\nSo k=6 doesn't work.\n\nThe only valid solution is: Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus\n\nANSWER: Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 5055, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 530, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 62, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1315, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5f0987e1-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423522-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:08-0500", "answer": "To determine how many times Friday the 13th occurs from January 1, 2026 through December 31, 2030, we can analyze each year in the given range. Here's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n### Key Observations:\n1. **Friday the 13th** occurs in a month if the 13th day of that month is a Friday.\n2. The Gregorian calendar repeats every 400 years, but for a small range like 2026\u20132030, we can check each year individually.\n3. The day of the week for the 13th can be calculated using Zeller's Congruence or by referencing known calendars.\n\n### Year-by-Year Analysis:\n\n#### 2026:\n- **February 13, 2026**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n- **March 13, 2026**: Friday (same as February due to February having 28 days in 2026)\n- **November 13, 2026**: Friday\n  **Total for 2026**: 3\n\n#### 2027:\n- **August 13, 2027**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n  **Total for 2027**: 1\n\n#### 2028:\n- **October 13, 2028**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n  **Total for 2028**: 1\n\n#### 2029:\n- **April 13, 2029**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n- **July 13, 2029**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n  **Total for 2029**: 2\n\n#### 2030:\n- **September 13, 2030**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n- **December 13, 2030**: Friday (1 occurrence)\n  **Total for 2030**: 2\n\n### Summing Up:\n- 2026: 3\n- 2027: 1\n- 2028: 1\n- 2029: 2\n- 2030: 2\n\n**Total occurrences from 2026 to 2030**: 3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9\n\nANSWER: 9"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-005", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Divisible by exactly two of 4, 6 and 9", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6542, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 457, "reasoning_tokens": 216, "input_tokens": 56, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 577, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "83334", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "83334"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5e4da9b2-d02a-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423520-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:08-0500", "answer": "Count each possible pair, excluding integers divisible by all three:\n\n- **4 and 6, but not 9:** multiples of 12 that are not multiples of 36:\n  \\[\n  \\left\\lfloor\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{12}\\right\\rfloor-\n  \\left\\lfloor\\frac{1{,}000{,}000}{36}\\right\\rfloor\n  =83{,}333-27{,}777=55{,}556.\n  \\]\n- **6 and 9, but not 4:** multiples of 18 that are not multiples of 36:\n  \\[\n  55{,}555-27{,}777=27{,}778.\n  \\]\n- **4 and 9, but not 6:** impossible, since every multiple of \\(\\operatorname{lcm}(4,9)=36\\) is divisible by 6.\n\nThus the total is \\(55{,}556+27{,}778=83{,}334\\).\n\nANSWER: 83334"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-004", "category": "reasoning", "title": "New York to London across the DST gap", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6322, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 571, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 112, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1519, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "14:30, 14:30, 14:30, 13:30, 14:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5ef016f2-d02a-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351e-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:09-0500", "answer": "To determine the London local times for the weekly meetings in New York, we need to account for the time difference between New York (Eastern Time, ET) and London (Greenwich Mean Time, GMT, or British Summer Time, BST, depending on the date).\n\nIn 2026:\n- **March 2, 9, and 16**: New York is on **Eastern Standard Time (EST, UTC-5)**, and London is on **Greenwich Mean Time (GMT, UTC+0)**. The time difference is **5 hours** (London is ahead).\n- **March 23 and 30**: New York switches to **Eastern Daylight Time (EDT, UTC-4)** on **March 8, 2026**, and London switches to **British Summer Time (BST, UTC+1)** on **March 29, 2026**. Thus:\n  - On **March 23**, New York is on EDT (UTC-4), and London is still on GMT (UTC+0). The time difference is **4 hours**.\n  - On **March 30**, both New York (EDT, UTC-4) and London (BST, UTC+1) have switched, so the time difference is **5 hours** (but London is now ahead by 5 hours due to BST).\n\nNow, converting **09:30 New York time** to London time for each date:\n\n1. **March 2 (EST, UTC-5; London GMT, UTC+0)**:\n   09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30** (London time).\n2. **March 9 (EST, UTC-5; London GMT, UTC+0)**:\n   09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30** (London time).\n3. **March 16 (EST, UTC-5; London GMT, UTC+0)**:\n   09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30** (London time).\n4. **March 23 (EDT, UTC-4; London GMT, UTC+0)**:\n   09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30** (London time).\n5. **March 30 (EDT, UTC-4; London BST, UTC+1)**:\n   09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30** (London time).\n\nANSWER: 14:30, 14:30, 14:30, 13:30, 14:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-003", "category": "reasoning", "title": "String rotate-and-shift trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6901, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 452, "reasoning_tokens": 282, "input_tokens": 124, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 400, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "kywasurrz", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "kywasurrz"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5edb4bd9-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351f-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:09-0500", "answer": "Each iteration first rearranges `s`, then shifts the characters at even indices (`0, 2, 4, 6, 8`) forward by `i` letters, wrapping around the alphabet.\n\n| `i` | After rearranging | After shifting |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `onductorc` | `pneudtprd` |\n| 2 | `eudtprdnp` | `guftrrfnr` |\n| 3 | `trrfnrfug` | `wrufqriuj` |\n| 4 | `qriujfurw` | `urmunfyra` |\n| 5 | `fyranumru` | `kywasurrz` |\n\nANSWER: kywasurrz"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-004", "category": "reasoning", "title": "New York to London across the DST gap", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 4251, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 233, "reasoning_tokens": 144, "input_tokens": 105, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 224, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5eefefdf-d02a-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351e-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:09-0500", "answer": "New York starts daylight saving time on March 8, 2026; London starts on March 29. London is therefore 5 hours ahead on March 2 and March 30, but 4 hours ahead on March 9, 16 and 23.\n\nANSWER: 14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-006", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four dice: max is twice the min", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 3616, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 302, "reasoning_tokens": 80, "input_tokens": 61, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 521, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "29/216", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "29/216"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5efa5595-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423521-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:11-0500", "answer": "Let the smallest value be \\(m\\). The largest must be \\(2m\\), so \\(m\\) can only be \\(1,2,\\) or \\(3\\).\n\nFor each \\(m\\), all four dice must lie in \\(\\{m,m+1,\\ldots,2m\\}\\), with both endpoints appearing. By inclusion-exclusion, the number of ordered outcomes is\n\\[\n(m+1)^4-2m^4+(m-1)^4.\n\\]\nThus the total number of favorable outcomes is\n\\[\n(2^4-2\\cdot1^4)+(3^4-2\\cdot2^4+1^4)\n+(4^4-2\\cdot3^4+2^4)\n=14+50+110=174.\n\\]\nThere are \\(6^4=1296\\) equally likely outcomes, giving\n\\[\n\\frac{174}{1296}=\\frac{29}{216}.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 29/216"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-008", "category": "reasoning", "title": "SLA deadline over the holidays", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 2964, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 169, "reasoning_tokens": 35, "input_tokens": 121, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 361, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "2026-12-30 12:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "2026-12-30 12:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5fdffaca-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d425c33-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:11-0500", "answer": "- **Thursday, December 24:** 16:30\u201317:00 counts as 0.5 business hours, leaving 19.5 hours.\n- **December 25\u201327:** Holiday and weekend; no hours count.\n- **Monday, December 28:** 8 hours count, leaving 11.5 hours.\n- **Tuesday, December 29:** 8 hours count, leaving 3.5 hours.\n- **Wednesday, December 30:** 3.5 hours after 09:00 is 12:30.\n\nANSWER: 2026-12-30 12:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-001", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Six-digit numbers with digit sum 27", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 10988, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1436, "reasoning_tokens": 1192, "input_tokens": 44, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 642, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 3333, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "55252", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "55252"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d7c40ea-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351c-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:11-0500", "answer": "Represent each integer from \\(1\\) to \\(999999\\) as a 6-digit string with leading zeros allowed. Then we need the number of 6-tuples of digits \\((d_1,\\dots,d_6)\\) with\n\n\\[\nd_1+\\cdots+d_6=27,\\qquad 0\\le d_i\\le 9.\n\\]\n\nWithout the upper bound, the number of nonnegative solutions is\n\n\\[\n\\binom{27+6-1}{6-1}=\\binom{32}{5}.\n\\]\n\nSubtract cases where some digit is at least 10, using inclusion-exclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{32}{5}\n-6\\binom{22}{5}\n+\\binom{6}{2}\\binom{12}{5}.\n\\]\n\nCompute:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{32}{5}=201376,\\quad\n\\binom{22}{5}=26334,\\quad\n\\binom{12}{5}=792.\n\\]\n\nSo the count is\n\n\\[\n201376-6(26334)+15(792)\n=201376-158004+11880\n=55252.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 55252"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-004", "category": "reasoning", "title": "New York to London across the DST gap", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 3498, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 453, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 103, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1303, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5eefefe1-d02a-11f1-ba7a-56adf5b8ca14", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351e-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:11-0500", "answer": "I need to find the London time for a 09:30 New York meeting on specific Mondays in March 2026.\n\nFirst, let me determine the time zone offsets. New York is in Eastern Time (ET), and London is in GMT/UTC (or BST during daylight saving).\n\n**Key dates for daylight saving time in 2026:**\n- US DST starts: Second Sunday in March = March 8, 2026 (clocks spring forward 1 hour)\n- UK BST starts: Last Sunday in March = March 29, 2026 (clocks spring forward 1 hour)\n\nSo the time difference changes during this period:\n- Before March 8: New York is EST (UTC-5), London is GMT (UTC+0) \u2192 5 hour difference\n- March 8 to March 28: New York is EDT (UTC-4), London is GMT (UTC+0) \u2192 4 hour difference\n- March 29 onwards: New York is EDT (UTC-4), London is BST (UTC+1) \u2192 5 hour difference\n\nNow let me calculate for each date:\n\n**March 2, 2026:** EST (UTC-5), London GMT (UTC+0). 09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30**\n\n**March 9, 2026:** EDT (UTC-4) [DST started March 8], London GMT (UTC+0). 09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30**\n\n**March 16, 2026:** EDT (UTC-4), London GMT (UTC+0). 09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30**\n\n**March 23, 2026:** EDT (UTC-4), London GMT (UTC+0). 09:30 + 4 hours = **13:30**\n\n**March 30, 2026:** EDT (UTC-4), London BST (UTC+1) [BST started March 29]. 09:30 + 5 hours = **14:30**\n\nANSWER: 14:30, 13:30, 13:30, 13:30, 14:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-008", "category": "reasoning", "title": "SLA deadline over the holidays", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 3299, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 408, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 119, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1110, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "2026-12-30 12:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "2026-12-30 12:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5fdffac9-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d425c33-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:11-0500", "answer": "I need to find the deadline 20 business hours after Thursday, December 24, 2026 at 16:30.\n\nFirst, let me establish the business hours: Monday-Friday, 09:00-17:00 (8 hours per day).\n\nHolidays: December 25, 2026 (Friday) and January 1, 2027 (Friday).\n\nStarting point: Thursday, December 24, 2026 at 16:30.\n\n**Thursday, December 24, 2026:**\n- From 16:30 to 17:00 = 0.5 hours remaining\n- Hours used: 0.5\n- Hours remaining: 19.5\n\n**Friday, December 25, 2026:** Holiday - no business hours\n\n**Saturday, December 26, 2026:** Weekend - no business hours\n\n**Sunday, December 27, 2026:** Weekend - no business hours\n\n**Monday, December 28, 2026:**\n- Full day: 09:00 to 17:00 = 8 hours\n- Hours used: 8\n- Total used: 8.5\n- Hours remaining: 11.5\n\n**Tuesday, December 29, 2026:**\n- Full day: 8 hours\n- Hours used: 8\n- Total used: 16.5\n- Hours remaining: 3.5\n\n**Wednesday, December 30, 2026:**\n- Need 3.5 hours\n- Start at 09:00, add 3.5 hours = 12:30\n- Hours used: 3.5\n- Total used: 20 \u2713\n\nLet me verify: 0.5 + 8 + 8 + 3.5 = 20 business hours \u2713\n\nThe deadline is Wednesday, December 30, 2026 at 12:30.\n\nANSWER: 2026-12-30 12:30"}
