{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-002", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Seven-seat row logic puzzle", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11822, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 626, "reasoning_tokens": 512, "input_tokens": 172, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 363, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5d69f15a-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351d-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:12-0500", "answer": "The seating order satisfies all the clues:\n- Ben (3) and Dev (6) have exactly two people between them.\n- Ana (4) and Eli (1) have exactly two people between them.\n- Ana (4) sits immediately left of Fay (5), with Cai (2) before Fay and Dev (6) after her.\n- Gus (7) sits to the right of Eli (1), and they are not adjacent.\n\nANSWER: Eli, Cai, Ben, Ana, Fay, Dev, Gus"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 9719, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 459, "reasoning_tokens": 333, "input_tokens": 55, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 310, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5f09aeef-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423522-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:12-0500", "answer": "The Friday the 13ths in the inclusive date range are:\n\n| Year | Months with Friday the 13th | Count |\n|---|---|---:|\n| 2026 | February, March, November | 3 |\n| 2027 | August | 1 |\n| 2028 | October | 1 |\n| 2029 | April, July | 2 |\n| 2030 | September, December | 2 |\n\nTotal: 3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = **9**.\n\nANSWER: 9"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-006", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four dice: max is twice the min", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 4892, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 369, "reasoning_tokens": 152, "input_tokens": 61, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 558, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "29/216", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "29/216"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5efa5596-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423521-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:12-0500", "answer": "There are \\(6^4=1296\\) equally likely ordered outcomes.\n\nIf the smallest value is \\(m\\), the largest must be \\(2m\\), so \\(m\\) can only be \\(1,2,\\) or \\(3\\). For each case, all dice must lie between the endpoints, and both endpoints must appear. By inclusion\u2013exclusion:\n\n- Minimum \\(1\\), maximum \\(2\\): \\(2^4-2(1^4)=14\\).\n- Minimum \\(2\\), maximum \\(4\\): \\(3^4-2(2^4)+1^4=50\\).\n- Minimum \\(3\\), maximum \\(6\\): \\(4^4-2(3^4)+2^4=110\\).\n\nThese cases are disjoint, so the probability is\n\\[\n\\frac{14+50+110}{1296}=\\frac{174}{1296}=\\frac{29}{216}.\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 29/216"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-003", "category": "reasoning", "title": "String rotate-and-shift trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11273, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 908, "reasoning_tokens": 715, "input_tokens": 124, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 464, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "kywasurrz", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "kywasurrz"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5edb4bd8-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351f-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:14-0500", "answer": "Each iteration moves the first `i` characters to the end in reverse order, then shifts the letters at indices `0, 2, 4, 6, 8` forward by `i`, wrapping around the alphabet.\n\nStarting with `s = \"conductor\"`:\n\n| `i` | After slicing and reversing | After shifting |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `onductorc` | `pneudtprd` |\n| 2 | `eudtprdnp` | `guftrrfnr` |\n| 3 | `trrfnrfug` | `wrufqriuj` |\n| 4 | `qriujfurw` | `urmunfyra` |\n| 5 | `fyranumru` | `kywasurrz` |\n\nANSWER: kywasurrz"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-008", "category": "reasoning", "title": "SLA deadline over the holidays", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 4996, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 542, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 137, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 1425, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "2026-12-30 12:30", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "2026-12-29 12:30"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5fe021dc-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d425c33-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:14-0500", "answer": "Let's calculate the resolution deadline step by step.\n\n### Given:\n- **Ticket opened:** Thursday, December 24, 2026 at 16:30\n- **Resolution deadline:** 20 business hours later\n- **Business hours:** Monday to Friday, 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours per day)\n- **Holidays:** December 25, 2026 (Friday) and January 1, 2027 (Friday) (no business hours)\n\n### Step 1: Calculate remaining business hours on December 24, 2026 (Thursday)\n- Business hours on December 24: 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours)\n- Ticket opened at 16:30, so remaining business hours on December 24:\n  - 17:00 - 16:30 = 0.5 hours\n\n### Step 2: Subtract remaining hours from the deadline\n- Remaining business hours to count: 20 - 0.5 = 19.5 hours\n\n### Step 3: Move to the next business day (December 28, 2026, Monday)\n- December 25 (Friday) is a holiday, so the next business day is December 28 (Monday).\n- Business hours on December 28: 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours)\n- Subtract 8 hours from the remaining 19.5 hours: 19.5 - 8 = 11.5 hours\n\n### Step 4: Move to the next business day (December 29, 2026, Tuesday)\n- Business hours on December 29: 09:00 to 17:00 (8 hours)\n- Subtract 8 hours from the remaining 11.5 hours: 11.5 - 8 = 3.5 hours\n\n### Step 5: Calculate the deadline on December 29, 2026\n- The remaining 3.5 hours are added to the start of business hours on December 29.\n- 09:00 + 3.5 hours = 12:30\n\n### Final Deadline:\n- December 29, 2026 at 12:30\n\nANSWER: 2026-12-29 12:30"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-003", "category": "reasoning", "title": "String rotate-and-shift trace", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": false, "score": 0.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11592, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1676, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 123, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 3098, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 0.0, "pass": false, "expected": "kywasurrz", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "kywksdipb"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5edb4bdb-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d42351f-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:14-0500", "answer": "I need to trace this program step by step.\n\nInitial: `s = \"conductor\"` (indices: c=0, o=1, n=2, d=3, u=4, c=5, t=6, o=7, r=8)\n\n**Iteration i=1:**\n- `s = s[1:] + s[:1][::-1]` = `\"onductor\" + \"c\"` = `\"onductorc\"`\n- Now apply Caesar shift by i=1 to even indices (j=0,2,4,6,8):\n  - j=0: 'o' \u2192 'p'\n  - j=1: 'n' (unchanged)\n  - j=2: 'd' \u2192 'e'\n  - j=3: 'u' (unchanged)\n  - j=4: 'c' \u2192 'd'\n  - j=5: 't' (unchanged)\n  - j=6: 'o' \u2192 'p'\n  - j=7: 'r' (unchanged)\n  - j=8: 'c' \u2192 'd'\n- `s = \"pnedudprd\"`\n\n**Iteration i=2:**\n- `s = s[2:] + s[:2][::-1]` = `\"edudprd\" + \"pn\"` = `\"edudprdpn\"`\n- Shift by i=2 on even indices (j=0,2,4,6,8):\n  - j=0: 'e' \u2192 'g'\n  - j=1: 'd' (unchanged)\n  - j=2: 'u' \u2192 'w'\n  - j=3: 'd' (unchanged)\n  - j=4: 'p' \u2192 'r'\n  - j=5: 'r' (unchanged)\n  - j=6: 'd' \u2192 'f'\n  - j=7: 'p' (unchanged)\n  - j=8: 'n' \u2192 'p'\n- `s = \"gdwd rfrfp\"` wait let me recheck: `\"gdwdrfrfp\"`\n\nLet me recheck: positions are g(0), d(1), w(2), d(3), r(4), r(5), f(6), p(7), p(8)? No wait, let me recheck the string.\n\n`s = \"edudprdpn\"` \u2014 that's 9 characters: e,d,u,d,p,r,d,p,n\n\n- j=0: 'e' \u2192 'g'\n- j=1: 'd' stays\n- j=2: 'u' \u2192 'w'\n- j=3: 'd' stays\n- j=4: 'p' \u2192 'r'\n- j=5: 'r' stays\n- j=6: 'd' \u2192 'f'\n- j=7: 'p' stays\n- j=8: 'n' \u2192 'p'\n\nSo `s = \"gdwdrfrfp\"`? That's g,d,w,d,r,r,f,p,p \u2014 wait that's wrong. Let me recheck: j=5 is 'r', j=6 is 'd'\u2192'f', j=7 is 'p', j=8 is 'n'\u2192'p'.\n\nSo: g, d, w, d, r, r, f, p, p = `\"gdwdrrfpp\"`? No wait, let me be careful. Original: e(0), d(1), u(2), d(3), p(4), r(5), d(6), p(7), n(8)\n\nAfter: g(0), d(1), w(2), d(3), r(4), r(5), f(6), p(7), p(8) = `\"gdwdrrfpp\"`\n\nHmm, that's 9 chars. Let me verify: g-d-w-d-r-r-f-p-p. Yes.\n\n**Iteration i=3:**\n- `s = s[3:] + s[:3][::-1]` = `\"drrfpp\" + \"wdg\"` = `\"drrfppwdg\"`\n- Shift by i=3 on even indices (j=0,2,4,6,8):\n  - j=0: 'd' \u2192 'g'\n  - j=1: 'r' (unchanged)\n  - j=2: 'r' \u2192 'u'\n  - j=3: 'f' (unchanged)\n  - j=4: 'p' \u2192 's'\n  - j=5: 'p' (unchanged)\n  - j=6: 'w' \u2192 'z'\n  - j=7: 'd' (unchanged)\n  - j=8: 'g' \u2192 'j'\n- `s = \"grufspzdj\"`\n\n**Iteration i=4:**\n- `s = s[4:] + s[:4][::-1]` = `\"spzdj\" + \"fur g\"` wait, `s[:4]` = `\"gruf\"`, reversed = `\"furg\"`\n- So `s = \"spzdj\" + \"furg\"` = `\"spzdjfurg\"`\n- Shift by i=4 on even indices (j=0,2,4,6,8):\n  - j=0: 's' \u2192 'w'\n  - j=1: 'p' (unchanged)\n  - j=2: 'z' \u2192 'd' (z=25, 25+4=29, 29%26=3, 'd')\n  - j=3: 'd' (unchanged)\n  - j=4: 'j' \u2192 'n'\n  - j=5: 'f' (unchanged)\n  - j=6: 'u' \u2192 'y'\n  - j=7: 'r' (unchanged)\n  - j=8: 'g' \u2192 'k'\n- `s = \"wpddnyyrk\"`? Let me check: w,p,d,d,n,f,y,r,k = `\"wpddnfyrk\"`\n\nWait: j=5 is 'f' (unchanged), j=6 is 'u'\u2192'y', j=7 is 'r', j=8 is 'g'\u2192'k'.\n\nSo: w(0), p(1), d(2), d(3), n(4), f(5), y(6), r(7), k(8) = `\"wpddnfyrk\"`\n\n**Iteration i=5:**\n- `s = s[5:] + s[:5][::-1]` = `\"fyrk\" + \"nddpw\"` = `\"fyrknddpw\"`\n- Shift by i=5 on even indices (j=0,2,4,6,8):\n  - j=0: 'f' \u2192 'k'\n  - j=1: 'y' (unchanged)\n  - j=2: 'r' \u2192 'w'\n  - j=3: 'k' (unchanged)\n  - j=4: 'n' \u2192 's'\n  - j=5: 'd' (unchanged)\n  - j=6: 'd' \u2192 'i'\n  - j=7: 'p' (unchanged)\n  - j=8: 'w' \u2192 'b' (w=22, 22+5=27, 27%26=1, 'b')\n- `s = \"kywksdipb\"`\n\nLet me verify: k(0), y(1), w(2), k(3), s(4), d(5), i(6), p(7), b(8) = `\"kywksdipb\"`\n\nANSWER: kywksdipb"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-006", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Four dice: max is twice the min", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_off", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning off)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "none", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 6856, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 853, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 61, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 2210, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "29/216", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "29/216"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5efa2e83-d02a-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423521-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:14-0500", "answer": "I need to find the probability that when rolling four fair six-sided dice, the largest value is exactly twice the smallest value.\n\n## Setting Up the Problem\n\nLet me denote the smallest value as $s$ and the largest value as $L$. We need $L = 2s$.\n\nSince dice show values 1-6, possible pairs $(s, L)$ where $L = 2s$:\n- $s = 1, L = 2$\n- $s = 2, L = 4$\n- $s = 3, L = 6$\n\nNote: $s = 4$ would give $L = 8$, impossible. So only three cases.\n\n## Counting Valid Outcomes for Each Case\n\nFor each case, I need: all four dice show values in $[s, 2s]$, with at least one die showing $s$ and at least one die showing $2s$.\n\n**Case 1: $s = 1, L = 2$** (values in $\\{1, 2\\}$, at least one 1 and at least one 2)\n\nTotal sequences with values in $\\{1,2\\}$: $2^4 = 16$\n- All 1s: 1 way (no 2, invalid)\n- All 2s: 1 way (no 1, invalid)\n\nValid: $16 - 2 = 14$\n\n**Case 2: $s = 2, L = 4$** (values in $\\{2, 3, 4\\}$, at least one 2 and at least one 4)\n\nTotal sequences with values in $\\{2,3,4\\}$: $3^4 = 81$\n\nUsing inclusion-exclusion for \"at least one 2 and at least one 4\":\n- Subtract sequences with no 2: values in $\\{3,4\\}$, so $2^4 = 16$\n- Subtract sequences with no 4: values in $\\{2,3\\}$, so $2^4 = 16$\n- Add back sequences with neither 2 nor 4: values in $\\{3\\}$, so $1^4 = 1$\n\nValid: $81 - 16 - 16 + 1 = 50$\n\n**Case 3: $s = 3, L = 6$** (values in $\\{3, 4, 5, 6\\}$, at least one 3 and at least one 6)\n\nTotal sequences with values in $\\{3,4,5,6\\}$: $4^4 = 256$\n\n- Subtract sequences with no 3: values in $\\{4,5,6\\}$, so $3^4 = 81$\n- Subtract sequences with no 6: values in $\\{3,4,5\\}$, so $3^4 = 81$\n- Add back sequences with neither 3 nor 6: values in $\\{4,5\\}$, so $2^4 = 16$\n\nValid: $256 - 81 - 81 + 16 = 110$\n\n## Total Valid Outcomes\n\nTotal valid: $14 + 50 + 110 = 174$\n\nTotal possible outcomes: $6^4 = 1296$\n\n## Probability\n\n$$\\frac{174}{1296} = \\frac{87}{648} = \\frac{29}{216}$$\n\nCheck: $174 = 2 \\times 87 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 29 = 6 \\times 29$\n$1296 = 6^4 = 1296 = 16 \\times 81 = 2^4 \\times 3^4$\n\nGCD of 174 and 1296: $174 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 29$, $1296 = 2^4 \\times 3^4$\nGCD = $2 \\times 3 = 6$\n\n$\\frac{174}{1296} = \\frac{29}{216}$\n\nCheck if 29/216 is reduced: 29 is prime, and $216 = 2^3 \\times 3^3$, so yes.\n\nANSWER: 29/216"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-007", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Friday the 13ths in 2026-2030", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 7058, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 334, "reasoning_tokens": 199, "input_tokens": 55, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 349, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "9", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "9"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5f0987e0-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423522-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:15-0500", "answer": "A month has a Friday the 13th exactly when it begins on a Sunday. The qualifying months are:\n\n| Year | Months with Friday the 13th | Count |\n|---|---|---:|\n| 2026 | February, March, November | 3 |\n| 2027 | August | 1 |\n| 2028 | October | 1 |\n| 2029 | April, July | 2 |\n| 2030 | September, December | 2 |\n\nTotal: \\(3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9\\).\n\nANSWER: 9"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "reasoning-005", "category": "reasoning", "title": "Divisible by exactly two of 4, 6 and 9", "difficulty": "hard", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 14990, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": null, "output_tokens": 1859, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 60, "search_tokens": null, "cached_input_tokens": 0}, "answer_chars": 4838, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "pass": true, "expected": "83334", "answer_line_found": true, "extracted": "83334"}, "workflow_id": "0sfj5e4da9b3-d02a-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfj5d423520-d02a-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:13:17-0500", "answer": "Alright, I have a problem to solve:\n\n**How many integers n with 1 <= n <= 1,000,000 are divisible by exactly two of the three numbers 4, 6, and 9?**\n\nFirst, I need to understand what's being asked. I need to find all integers from 1 to 1,000,000 that are divisible by exactly two out of the three numbers: 4, 6, and 9.\n\n### Understanding the Problem\nI need numbers divisible by exactly two of these three numbers. That means:\n1. Divisible by 4 and 6 but not 9.\n2. Divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6.\n3. Divisible by 6 and 9 but not 4.\n\n### Approach\nI think I need to find the count of numbers divisible by any two numbers but not the third. To do this, I can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\n### Step 1: Find Numbers Divisible by Pairs\nFirst, I need to find numbers divisible by any two of the numbers.\n\n#### 1. Divisible by 4 and 6\nA number divisible by both 4 and 6 must be divisible by the least common multiple (LCM) of 4 and 6.\n\n- LCM of 4 and 6:\n  - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n  - Prime factors of 6: 2 * 3\n  - LCM is the highest power of each prime: 2^2 * 3 = 12\n\nSo, numbers divisible by both 4 and 6 are divisible by 12.\n\n#### 2. Divisible by 4 and 9\n- LCM of 4 and 9:\n  - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n  - Prime factors of 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\nNumbers divisible by both 4 and 9 are divisible by 36.\n\n#### 3. Divisible by 6 and 9\n- LCM of 6 and 9:\n  - Prime factors of 6: 2 * 3\n  - Prime factors of 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2 * 3^2 = 18\n\nNumbers divisible by both 6 and 9 are divisible by 18.\n\n### Step 2: Find Numbers Divisible by All Three\nBut the problem asks for numbers divisible by exactly two of the three numbers. So, I need to exclude numbers divisible by all three.\n\n- LCM of 4, 6, and 9:\n  - Prime factors of 4: 2^2\n  - Prime factors of 6: 2 * 3\n  - Prime factors of 9: 3^2\n  - LCM: 2^2 * 3^2 = 36\n\nNumbers divisible by all three are divisible by 36.\n\n### Step 3: Count Numbers Divisible by Each Pair but Not All Three\nNow, I need to find numbers divisible by each pair but not by all three.\n\n#### 1. Divisible by 4 and 6 but not 9\n- Numbers divisible by 12 (LCM of 4 and 6).\n- But not divisible by 36 (LCM of 4,6,9).\n\nFirst, find the count of numbers divisible by 12 up to 1,000,000:\n- Total numbers divisible by 12: floor(1,000,000 / 12) = 83,333.\n\nNow, find the count of numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000:\n- Total numbers divisible by 36: floor(1,000,000 / 36) = 27,777.\n\nNow, subtract the second count from the first to get numbers divisible by 12 but not by 36:\n- 83,333 - 27,777 = 55,556.\n\n#### 2. Divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6\n- Numbers divisible by 36 (LCM of 4 and 9).\n- But not divisible by 36 and 6, which is the same as not divisible by the LCM of 36 and 6.\n\nBut, the LCM of 36 and 6 is 36, because 36 is already divisible by 6.\n\nBut, if a number is divisible by 36, it's automatically divisible by 6. So, the condition \"divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6\" is not possible because any number divisible by 36 must be divisible by 6.\n\nTherefore, the count is zero.\n\n#### 3. Divisible by 6 and 9 but not 4\n- Numbers divisible by 18 (LCM of 6 and 9).\n- But not divisible by 36 (LCM of 6,9,4).\n\nFirst, find the count of numbers divisible by 18 up to 1,000,000:\n- Total numbers divisible by 18: floor(1,000,000 / 18) \u2248 55,555.555 \u2192 55,555.\n\nNow, find the count of numbers divisible by 36 up to 1,000,000:\n- As before, 27,777.\n\nNow, subtract the second count from the first to get numbers divisible by 18 but not by 36:\n- 55,555 - 27,777 = 27,778.\n\n### Verification\nBut, I need to ensure that I'm not missing anything. The problem is about numbers divisible by exactly two of the three numbers.\n\n- For the first case (4 and 6 but not 9), it's correct because numbers divisible by 12 but not by 36 are divisible by 4 and 6 but not by 9.\n- For the second case, any number divisible by 4 and 9 must be divisible by 6, so it's automatically divisible by all three. Hence, no numbers satisfy the condition of being divisible by 4 and 9 but not 6.\n- For the third case, numbe"}
