{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "grounding-001", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 Nobel Prize in Physics", "difficulty": "easy", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_low", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning low)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "low", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 7481, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": 1, "output_tokens": 175, "reasoning_tokens": 47, "input_tokens": 13011, "search_tokens": 0, "cached_input_tokens": 4494}, "answer_chars": 581, "finish_reason": "completed", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "citation_count": 1, "pass": true, "pattern_misses": [], "expected": "Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory", "answer_line_found": true, "cited": true, "extracted": "Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjedddb241-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjed9e7d5b-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:31:29-0500", "answer": "**Francis Halzen** was awarded the **2026 Nobel Prize in Physics**, announced on October 6, 2026. The citation centers on the **IceCube Neutrino Observatory**: \u201cfor decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin.\u201d This is confirmed by the American Physical Society and Fermilab\u2019s reproduction of the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences\u2019 announcement. ([aps.org](https://www.aps.org/about/news/2026/10/aps-winner-2026-nobel-physics?utm_source=openai))\n\nANSWER: Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "grounding-002", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine", "difficulty": "medium", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l3", "config_label": "Mistral Large 3 (baseline)", "model": "mistral-large-2512", "reasoning_effort": null, "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 4381, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": 1, "output_tokens": 176, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 828, "search_tokens": 6820, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 452, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "citation_count": 6, "pass": true, "pattern_misses": [], "expected": "Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann, Georg Nagel; optogenetics (light-gated ion channels)", "answer_line_found": true, "cited": true, "extracted": "Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann, Georg Nagel; optogenetics and light-gated ion channels"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjefecf28f-d02c-11f1-aff9-321d2204384c", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjed9ecb7e-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:31:29-0500", "answer": "The 2026 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was awarded jointly to **Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann, and Georg Nagel** for their discoveries concerning light-gated ion channels and optogenetics. This groundbreaking work allows researchers to control the activity of individual nerve cells in the living brain using light, revolutionizing neuroscience.\n\nANSWER: Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann, Georg Nagel; optogenetics and light-gated ion channels"}
