{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "grounding-001", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 Nobel Prize in Physics", "difficulty": "easy", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "l4_high", "config_label": "Mistral Large 4 (reasoning high)", "model": "mistral-large-4", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11222, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": 3, "output_tokens": 1092, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 802, "search_tokens": 17431, "cached_input_tokens": null}, "answer_chars": 1887, "finish_reason": "stop", "thinking_chars": 1960, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "citation_count": 7, "pass": true, "pattern_misses": [], "expected": "Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory", "answer_line_found": true, "cited": true, "extracted": "Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjedddb240-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjed9e7d5b-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:31:33-0500", "answer": "I'll search for the 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics laureates and the observatory mentioned in the citation.The search results are consistent. Let me verify with the official Nobel Prize press release to confirm the exact citation.The 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics was announced on 6 October 2026 by the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences. It was awarded to **Francis Halzen** (born 1944 in Tienen, Belgium; Professor at the University of Wisconsin\u2013Madison, USA) with the citation:\n\n> \"for decisive contributions to the **IceCube Neutrino Observatory** and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin\"\n\nThe observatory at the heart of the citation is the **IceCube Neutrino Observatory** at the South Pole in Antarctica \u2014 a cubic kilometre of glacial ice instrumented with thousands of light sensors, which Halzen first proposed in 1988 and which was completed in 2011. IceCube detects high-energy cosmic neutrinos (\"ghost particles\") and opened the era of neutrino astronomy.\n\n**Sources used:**\n- NobelPrize.org, official press release (6 Oct 2026): https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2026/press-release/\n- NobelPrize.org, prize announcement & laureate facts: https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2026/prize-announcement/ and https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/physics/2026/halzen/facts/\n- Nature, \"Nobel physics prize awarded for detection of cosmic neutrinos\": https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-026-03092-1\n- PBS News / AP: https://www.pbs.org/newshour/science/watch-live-winner-of-the-2026-nobel-prize-in-physics-is\n- Scientific American: https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/2026-nobel-prize-in-physics-awarded-to-francis-halzen-for-discovery-of-high-energy-neutrinos/\n- Al Jazeera: https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/6/francis-halzen-wins-nobel-prize-in-physics-for-work-on-ghost-particles\n\nANSWER: Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory"}
{"run_id": "full-r3", "item_id": "grounding-001", "category": "grounding", "title": "2026 Nobel Prize in Physics", "difficulty": "easy", "repeat": 1, "config_id": "sol_high", "config_label": "GPT-6.1 Sol (reasoning high)", "model": "gpt-6.1-sol", "reasoning_effort": "high", "pass": true, "score": 1.0, "api_ok": true, "error": null, "latency_ms": 11718, "turns": 1, "retries": 0, "usage": {"search_calls": 2, "output_tokens": 406, "reasoning_tokens": 252, "input_tokens": 17587, "search_tokens": 0, "cached_input_tokens": 4494}, "answer_chars": 717, "finish_reason": "completed", "thinking_chars": 0, "detail": {"score": 1.0, "citation_count": 2, "pass": true, "pattern_misses": [], "expected": "Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory", "answer_line_found": true, "cited": true, "extracted": "Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory"}, "workflow_id": "0sfjedddb242-d02c-11f1-89ad-1a5d2f4e051b", "parent_workflow_id": "0sfjed9e7d5b-d02c-11f1-9071-46362399c705", "recorded_at": "2026-10-24T23:31:33-0500", "answer": "**Francis Halzen**, of the University of Wisconsin\u2013Madison, was awarded the **2026 Nobel Prize in Physics**, announced on October 6, 2026. The Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences\u2019 citation reads: \u201cfor decisive contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory and the discovery of high-energy neutrinos of astrophysical origin\u201d. ([kva.se](https://www.kva.se/en/news/the-nobel-prize-in-physics-2026/))\n\nThe observatory named in the citation is the **IceCube Neutrino Observatory**, located at the South Pole, as also confirmed by the U.S. National Science Foundation. ([nsf.gov](https://www.nsf.gov/mps/updates/2026-nobel-prize-physics-awarded-nsf-icecube-neutrino))\n\nANSWER: Francis Halzen; IceCube Neutrino Observatory"}
